                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Tucson AZ",
                "headline": "Flood Watch issued September 26 at 9:17PM MST until September 29 at 11:00PM MST by NWS Tucson AZ",
                "description": "* WHAT...Flash flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...For all of southeast Arizona, including the following\nareas, Baboquivari Mountains, Catalina and Rincon Mountains,\nChiricahua Mountains, Dragoon and Mule and Huachuca and Santa Rita\nMountains, Eastern Cochise County below 5000 feet, Galiuro and\nPinaleno Mountains, South Central Pinal County, Southeast Pinal\nCounty, Tohono O'odham Nation, Tucson Metro Area, Upper Gila River\nValley, Upper San Pedro River Valley, Upper Santa Cruz River\nValley/Altar Valley, Western Pima County and White Mountains of\nGraham and Greenlee Counties.\n\n* WHEN...From Monday morning through Tuesday evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Deep tropical moisture will combine with an approaching upper low\nand trough, resulting in areas of heavy rainfall. Rainfall totals\nof 1 to 2 inches are possible across most of southeast Arizona,\nwith locally heavier amounts under the stronger thunderstorms.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood",
                "instruction": "You should monitor later forecasts and be prepared to take action\nshould Flash Flood Warnings be issued.",
                "response": "Prepare",
                "note": null,
